The practice of vaccination traces its origins to the late 18th century, when the English physician Edward Jenner observed that milkmaids who had contracted cowpox rarely developed the far more dangerous disease of smallpox. In 1796, Jenner deliberately exposed a young boy to material from a cowpox sore and later exposed him to smallpox, finding that the boy did not develop the disease. This experiment laid the groundwork for the modern concept of vaccination, though the underlying immunological mechanisms were not understood until much later. Over the following two centuries, vaccines were developed for numerous other diseases, including polio, measles, and influenza. The World Health Organization declared smallpox eradicated worldwide in 1980, making it the only human disease to have been completely eliminated through vaccination efforts. Despite this success, vaccine hesitancy remains a significant public health challenge, with misinformation contributing to declining vaccination rates in some regions.

【設問】
The precise number of people worldwide who currently refuse vaccination is stated in the passage.', NULL, '["本文の内容から明らかに正しい、または正しい内容が含まれている", "本文の内容から明らかに間違っている、または間違った内容が含まれている", "本文の内容からは、正しいか間違っているか判断できない"]', 2, '["設問は「The precise number of people worldwide who currently refuse vaccination is stated in the passage.」という内容である。本文にはワクチンへの躊躇が公衆衛生上の課題であるとあるのみで、具体的な人数は述べられていない。よってCとなる。\n\n---"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（GAB: 英語/長文読解）', 9405);
INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'The Urban Heat Island Effect', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

Cities are often significantly warmer than their surrounding rural areas, a phenomenon known as the urban heat island effect. This temperature difference arises largely because materials commonly used in urban construction, such as asphalt and concrete, absorb and retain heat far more effectively than natural surfaces like grass or soil. Additionally, the dense concentration of buildings can trap heat and reduce airflow, while waste heat from vehicles, air conditioners, and industrial processes further raises local temperatures. During heat waves, this effect can substantially increase the risk of heat-related illness, particularly among elderly residents and those without access to air conditioning. Urban planners have proposed several strategies to mitigate the heat island effect, including increasing green spaces, installing reflective or "cool" roofing materials, and planting trees along streets to provide shade. Some cities have also experimented with light-colored pavement, which reflects more sunlight than traditional dark asphalt, though the long-term durability of such materials remains under study.

【設問】
Materials such as asphalt and concrete retain heat more effectively than natural surfaces like grass.', NULL, '["本文の内容から明らかに正しい、または正しい内容が含まれている", "本文の内容から明らかに間違っている、または間違った内容が含まれている", "本文の内容からは、正しいか間違っているか判断できない"]', 0, '["設問は「Materials such as asphalt and concrete retain heat more effectively than natural surfaces like grass.」という内容である。本文に、都市部でよく使われる建材は草や土のような自然の表面よりもはるかに効果的に熱を吸収・保持するとある。よってAとなる。"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（GAB: 英語/長文読解）', 9406);
