INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'Roman Road Engineering', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

The Roman road network, which eventually stretched over 250,000 miles across the empire, was renowned for its durability and engineering precision. Roman engineers typically constructed roads in multiple layers, beginning with a foundation of large stones, followed by smaller gravel, and finally a surface layer of tightly fitted paving stones. This layered design allowed for effective drainage, preventing water from pooling and weakening the road''s structure, a problem that had plagued earlier, less sophisticated roads. Many of these roads were built primarily for military purposes, enabling Roman legions to move quickly between distant provinces. However, the roads also facilitated trade and communication across the empire, contributing to economic integration among its diverse regions. Remarkably, several sections of Roman roads remain visible and even usable today, nearly two thousand years after their construction. Some modern highways in Europe still follow routes originally established by Roman engineers, a testament to their careful planning.

【設問】
