INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'Coral Reef Bleaching', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

Coral reefs, often called the rainforests of the sea, support an extraordinary diversity of marine life despite covering less than one percent of the ocean floor. Corals are colonies of tiny animals called polyps that form symbiotic relationships with microscopic algae living within their tissues. These algae provide corals with most of their energy through photosynthesis, while also giving reefs their vibrant colors. When ocean temperatures rise even slightly above normal for extended periods, corals expel these algae in a stress response known as bleaching, leaving the coral skeleton white and vulnerable. Prolonged bleaching events can lead to widespread coral death, as the coral loses its primary energy source. Major bleaching events driven by rising sea temperatures have affected large sections of the Great Barrier Reef in recent years. While some coral species have shown a limited ability to recover if temperatures return to normal quickly, scientists warn that repeated bleaching events leave reefs increasingly less resilient over time.

【設問】
