INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'Space Debris', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

As of recent estimates, hundreds of thousands of pieces of debris larger than one centimeter are orbiting the Earth, remnants of decades of space exploration including defunct satellites, spent rocket stages, and fragments from past collisions. Even small pieces of debris pose a serious threat to functioning spacecraft because objects in orbit travel at extremely high speeds, meaning even a tiny fragment can cause significant damage upon impact. This has led to growing concern among scientists about a scenario known as the Kessler syndrome, in which collisions between debris generate further debris, potentially triggering a cascading chain reaction that could render certain orbital regions unusable for generations. Several organizations have proposed methods for actively removing debris, including nets, harpoons, and robotic arms designed to capture defunct satellites and guide them toward reentry into the atmosphere. International cooperation on this issue remains limited, however, as no single treaty currently obligates nations to remove the debris their launches create.

【設問】
