Enhanced geothermal systems have occasionally been associated with minor seismic activity.', NULL, '["本文の内容から明らかに正しい、または正しい内容が含まれている", "本文の内容から明らかに間違っている、または間違った内容が含まれている", "本文の内容からは、正しいか間違っているか判断できない"]', 0, '["設問は「Enhanced geothermal systems have occasionally been associated with minor seismic activity.」という内容である。本文に、強化地熱システムが時折軽微な地震活動と関連付けられてきたとある。よってAとなる。\n\n---"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（GAB: 英語/長文読解）', 9423);
INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'The History of Paper Currency', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

Paper currency first emerged in China during the Tang dynasty, though it became more widely used centuries later under the Song dynasty, largely as a practical solution to the inconvenience of transporting large quantities of heavy metal coins over long distances. Merchants engaged in long-distance trade initially used paper certificates that could be exchanged for coins held on deposit, a system that gradually evolved into government-issued currency backed by the state. When Marco Polo traveled to China in the 13th century, he reportedly expressed astonishment at the widespread use of paper money, a concept still unfamiliar in Europe at the time. Paper currency did not become common in Europe until several centuries later, with various countries gradually adopting similar systems between the 17th and 18th centuries. Early paper currencies were often subject to significant inflation when governments printed excessive amounts to cover expenses, a problem that has recurred throughout history in various countries. Modern currencies, while no longer backed by physical commodities such as gold, rely on public confidence in the issuing government to maintain their value.

【設問】
