INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'Muscle Memory and Motor Learning', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

When a person learns a new physical skill, such as playing a musical instrument or a particular sport, repeated practice gradually allows certain movements to become automatic, a phenomenon commonly referred to as muscle memory, though the memory itself is actually stored within the brain and nervous system rather than in the muscles. Neuroscientists have found that repeated practice strengthens specific neural pathways associated with a given movement, allowing the brain to eventually execute the sequence with minimal conscious attention or effort. This process explains why skills such as riding a bicycle or typing on a keyboard, once thoroughly learned, tend to be retained for years even without regular practice, unlike purely factual information, which is more prone to being forgotten over time. Interestingly, research suggests that mentally rehearsing a physical movement, without actually performing it, can also strengthen the relevant neural pathways to a meaningful degree, a finding that has led some athletes and musicians to incorporate visualization exercises into their regular training routines. However, most researchers agree that actual physical practice remains considerably more effective than mental rehearsal alone for developing genuine motor proficiency.

