INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'Bat Echolocation', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

Bats that are active at night rely heavily on a biological sonar system known as echolocation to navigate their environment and locate flying insects in complete darkness. To echolocate, a bat emits high-frequency sound pulses, typically well beyond the range of human hearing, and then listens for the returning echoes as these sound waves bounce off nearby objects. By analyzing subtle differences in the timing, intensity, and frequency of the returning echoes, a bat''s brain can construct a remarkably detailed representation of its surroundings, allowing it to detect obstacles as thin as a human hair and track the erratic flight patterns of small flying insects with extraordinary precision. Interestingly, some species of moths that serve as common prey for bats have evolved specialized structures capable of detecting these ultrasonic pulses, allowing the moths to take evasive maneuvers or even emit their own sounds designed to jam the bat''s echolocation system. Researchers studying echolocation have drawn inspiration from these biological systems in developing improved sonar and radar technologies, an approach broadly referred to as biomimicry, in which engineers attempt to replicate solutions that have already been refined through millions of years of natural evolutionary processes.

【設問】
Engineers have developed improved sonar technologies by studying and drawing inspiration from bat echolocation.', NULL, '["本文の内容から明らかに正しい、または正しい内容が含まれている", "本文の内容から明らかに間違っている、または間違った内容が含まれている", "本文の内容からは、正しいか間違っているか判断できない"]', 0, '["設問は「Engineers have developed improved sonar technologies by studying and drawing inspiration from bat echolocation.」という内容である。本文に、研究者たちがコウモリのエコーロケーションからヒントを得てソナーやレーダー技術の改良に取り組んでいるとある。よってAとなる。\n\n---"]', 4, 'published', 'ユーザー提供のオリジナル問題（GAB: 英語/長文読解）', 9449);
INSERT INTO questions (category, sub_category, title, question_text, tables_json, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, status, originality_note, passage_group_id) VALUES ('gab_english', '長文読解', 'The Library of Alexandria', '次の文章を読み、設問の内容が本文から見て「正しい」「間違っている」「どちらとも言えない」のいずれであるかを判断しなさい。

The Library of Alexandria, founded in Egypt sometime in the early 3rd century BCE under the patronage of the Ptolemaic dynasty, is often remembered as one of the largest and most significant repositories of knowledge in the ancient world, reportedly housing hundreds of thousands of scrolls covering a vast range of subjects. Scholars affiliated with the library, which functioned as part of a broader research institution known as the Mouseion, made significant contributions to fields including mathematics, astronomy, and medicine, attracting intellectuals from across the Mediterranean world. Contrary to a popular but historically oversimplified narrative, the library''s decline appears to have resulted not from a single catastrophic fire but rather from a gradual process involving multiple episodes of damage, political instability, and reduced funding that unfolded over several centuries. Because so few written records from the library''s own collection survived these events, much of what modern historians know about its holdings and daily operations must be pieced together from secondhand references made by ancient authors writing about the institution rather than from direct archaeological evidence. The library''s eventual disappearance is frequently cited as a symbol of the fragility of accumulated human knowledge.

【設問】
